## ABSTRACT
The adsorbents–adsorbates interaction is critical for resourcelization in heavy metal wastewater treatment. Nevertheless, it is still indistinct to depict the impact of metal center effect on heavy metals removal performance in metal-organic frameworks (MOFs)-based adsorbents. Herein, a series of MOFs with different metal centers of Mg(II), La(III), and Zr(IV) are rationally designed, and the effect of electronic structure on the Sb(V) removal performance is systematically investigated. The obtained La-MGs achieve Sb(V) adsorption capacity of \(897.6~\mathrm{mg/g}\), which is about 1.2 and 4.5 times above average than those of Zr-MGs and Mg-MGs, respectively. On account of more edge adsorption sites achieve, the sites utilization efficiency of La-MGs \((92.1\%)\) is much better than Zr-MGs \((75.0\%)\) and Mg-MGs \((20.4\%)\). Furthermore, density functional theory (DFT) calculations reveal that La-MGs are more active than Mg-MGs and Zr-MGs, owing to the lower adsorption energy, higher charge transfer, and stronger bonding interaction, which will promote the Sb(V) removal performance. The experimental results in practical water indicate that La-MGs effectively capture antimony at low concentration, reaching drinking water standard in samples from Ganjiang River. This study opens an avenue for atomic-level insight into high-efficient adsorbents design for water treatment from electronic structure-modification of active centers.
## KEYWORDS
metal-organic frameworks, metal center effect, electronic structure, theoretical calculation, Sb(V) removal
## 1 Introduction
Adsorption-based technology offers attractive means for producing renewable water and metal resources, which becomes promising for schemes of green hills and clear waters owing to its robust usage and high efficiency [1–3]. Recently, the environmental pollution and ecological toxicity caused by the emerging contaminant antimony (Sb) has received global concerns. Considering the potentially high toxicity and health risks, the US Environmental Protection Agency has classified Sb and its compounds as priority pollutants and regulated the maximum contaminant level (MCL) in drinking water as \(6~\upmu\mathrm{g/L}\) [4]. Therefore, the effective removal of Sb ions from drinking water is essential to ensure the health and life quality for millions of people under Sb-contamination threats. Diverse adsorbents including Mg-based, Zr-based, and bimetal oxides have been regarded as promising alternatives for Sb(V) removal [5–7]. Among them, Zr-based compounds exhibit superior Sb(V) adsorption performance [7]. Despite significant advances achieved on Sb(V) removal with metal-based adsorbents, scalable applications are still hampered by the lower active sites-density as well as weak bonding interaction, which are determined by the electronic structure of active sites. Hence, exploring the tailored structure and understanding the impact of electronic structure on the adsorption performance is a desirable solution to cope with these issues.
