To meet the target, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), a class of crystalline porous materials featuring intriguing structures and abundant active centers, would be ideal alternatives for exploring structure–property–performance relationships [8–14]. In recent years, there have been quite a few reports on the performance enhancement guided material structures design, most of which concern about the defect–performance relationships for heavy metals removal [15–20]. In fact, the electronic structure around metal active centers can be easily regulated in catalytic region to improve the activity via the linker functional group alteration or metal substitution in MOFs [21–24]. Although a few recent investigations on electronic structures of MOFs have suggested preliminary influences on catalysis, to the best of our knowledge, there are still few systematical explorations to clarify the effects of
different electronic structure of metal active centers in MOFs on the adsorption performance.
To cope with these issues, metal center with different content of d orbital electrons (e.g., zero, one, and two) and diverse valence (e.g., bivalent, trivalent, and tetravalent metal cations) were employed. Based on above results and our previous study about the relation between electronic structure and heavy metal adsorption performance (e.g., As(III) [25] and Sb(V) [26]), \(\mathrm{Mg}\) without d orbital electron and displaying bivalent, La with one-d orbital electron and displaying trivalent, and \(Z\mathbf{r}\) possessing two-d orbital electron and tetravalent were chosen as coordination centers. Three MOFs-based adsorbents (i.e., Mg-MGs, La-MGs, and La-MGs) via the ultrasound-assisted solvent approach (Scheme 1) were constructed. The micromorphology, crystal phase, crystal structure, and adsorption sites have been carefully characterized. The behaviors of Sb(V) captured by the as-synthesized MOFs adsorbents have been investigated by batch experiments including isothermal adsorption test and dynamic experiments. Further, the relationships between adsorption performance and structure properties have been explored and established. In addition, density functional theory (DFT) calculations were employed to provide a deeper insight into the distinct adsorption performance of Sb(V) with three MOFs-based adsorbents through metal centers effect. Finally, to evaluate the potential of La-MGs-based adsorbents for practical water treatment, experiments were conducted with water samples supplied from the Tianyi Lake and Ganjiang River in China.
