The details of preparation of Mg-MGs, La-MGs, and \(Z\mathbf{r}\) -MGs were readily provided in Section S2 in the ESM, and batch adsorption experiments including adsorption modeling and thermodynamics equations were provided in Section S3 in the ESM.
## 2.3 Theoretical computation
All the calculations were performed within the framework of the DFT as implemented in the Vienna ab initio software package (VASP 5.3.5) code within the Perdew–Burke–Ernzerhof (PBE) generalized gradient approximation and the projected– augmented–wave (PAW) method [27–30]. The cutoff energy for the plane-wave basis set was set to be \(400~\mathrm{eV}\) . The Brillouin zone of the surface unit cell was sampled by Monkhorst–Pack (MP) grids for MOF structures optimizations. The MOFs clusters were determined by \(1\times1\times1\) MP grid. The convergence criterion for the electronic self-consistent iteration and force was set as \(10^{-5}\;\mathrm{eV}\) and \(0.01\,\mathrm{eV/\mathring{A}}\) , respectively.
The adsorption energy \((E_{\mathrm{ads}})\) of the surface species is defined by Eq. (1)
\[
E_{\mathrm{ads}}=E_{\mathrm{total}}-E_{\mathrm{surface}}-E_{\mathrm{species}}
\]
where \(E_{\mathrm{total}}\) represents the total energy of the adsorbed species with catalyst surface, \(E_{\mathrm{surface}}\) is the energy of the empty catalysts surface, and \(E_{\mathrm{species}}\) is the energy of the species in the gas phase.
## 3 Results and discussion
## 3.1 The construction of three MOFs adsorbents with different coordination environment
As presented in Fig. 1(a), the similar synthesis process resulted in the distinguishably different structure of La-MGs to Mg-MGs and Zr-MGs, due to the different capability to form a complex with the different coordination metal centers during the initial synthesis process. Mg and La ions obtained larger sized petal-like structures and ribbon-like structures, respectively, whereas \(Z\mathbf{r}\) ions possessed smaller sized cumulated cubic appearance. The elemental mappings in Figs. S1(a)–S1(c) in the ESM revealed the homogeneous distribution of Mg, La, Zr, and O elements on the entire nanostructure. The X–ray diffraction (XRD) measurement [31] demonstrated that the diffraction peak of La-MGs mainly located at \(8^{\circ}\) , \(13^{\circ}\) , and \(20^{\circ}\) (Fig. S2(a) in the ESM), whereas different diffraction peaks appeared in (Mg-MGs \((7^{\circ},\,20,\) and \(22^{\circ}\) ) and \(Z\mathbf{r}\) -MGs \((10^{\circ},\,16^{\circ},\) and \(17^{\circ}\) ), which may affect the electronic structure of metal centers. For an in-depth understanding, the XRD results were calculation from Riveted refinement by Materials Studio and Bruker TOPAS. The results indicated that the coordination number of La and \(Z\mathbf{r}\) was 8 in the absorbents,
whereas the coordination number of \(\mathrm{Mg}\) was 4. It was reported that the saturated coordination number of La, Zr, and \(\mathrm{Mg}\) was 9, 8, and 4, respectively [7, 32, 33] (Fig. 1(d)), which was beneficial for the formation of a monodentate-mononuclear adsorption structure to promote the \(\operatorname{Sb}(\mathrm{V})\) adsorption process for La-MGs. While for Mg-MGs and Zr-MGs, the ligand would compete the metal centers with \(\operatorname{Sb}(\mathrm{V})\) and was not conducive to form fine structural configuration with \(\operatorname{Sb}(\mathrm{V})\). According to the calculated results, La-MGs exhibited denser absorption sites distributed in edge regions than that of Mg-MGs and Zr-MGs, which is in favor of capturing \(\operatorname{Sb}(\mathrm{V})\) with plane structure theoretically (Figs. 1(b) and 1(c)) and Table S1 in the ESM). In addition, thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) plots showed weight losses of water molecule about \(15\%\) in the temperature range of \(50{-}350\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). From \(350{-}600\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), the percentages of weight loss of linker were much larger and with values of \(25.2\%\) , \(27.7\%\) , and \(32.6\%\) for Mg-MGs, La-MGs, and Zr-MGs, respectively [34]. More importantly, there is a clear trend among the samples: the magnitude of the decomposition weight loss systematically increased with the increasing amounts of di-orbital electrons of metal centers (Fig. S2(a) in the ESM). Besides, the remarkable enhanced permeability and reaction (EPR) signal of three adsorbents appeared at about \(3{,}510\ \mathrm{G}\) was ascribed to oxygen vacancies for the value of \(g\) was 2,002 [35], but the peak intensity differed greatly (Fig. S2(b) in the ESM). These results suggested that the metal center diversities would affect the morphologies and distribution of metal centers for the as-synthesized adsorbents.
## 3.2 The impact of coordination environment on Sb(V) adsorption
Depending on the recent research [1, 5, 36–44] (Fig. 2(a)), the diversity of metal centers and coordination environment of
adsorption sites had a crucial effect on the adsorption performance for \(\operatorname{Sb}(\mathrm{V})\) removal. The effects of metal centers on \(\operatorname{Sb}(\mathrm{V})\) removal performance in our study were evaluated by the batch experiments. The \(\mathrm{pH}\) is known to have an impact on the surface charge of the adsorbent and the adsorbate. The effect of \(\mathrm{pH}\) on the adsorption capacity of \(\operatorname{Sb}(\mathrm{V})\) on three adsorbents is shown in Fig. S3 in the ESM. In Fig. S4(a) in the ESM, the adsorption capacity of \(\operatorname{Sb}(\mathrm{V})\) was relatively stable and only fluctuated slightly over a wide range of \(\mathrm{pH}\) values from 2.0 to 7.0 for three adsorbents, and the maximum adsorption capacity for \(\operatorname{Sb}(\mathrm{V})\) was obtained when the \(\mathrm{pH}\) value was approximately 3.0. The difference in adsorption performance for \(\operatorname{Sb}(\mathrm{V})\) may be due to electrostatic force differences that existed between the three adsorbents surface and Sb oxyanions presented in the solutions. Zeta-potential measurements showed that the surfaces of untreated three adsorbents have net positive charge for three adsorbents (Mg-MGs: \(15.6\ \mathrm{mV}\), La-MGs: \(6.5\ \mathrm{mV}\), and Zr-MGs: \(43.8\ \mathrm{mV}\)) at \(\mathrm{pH}\ 3\) (Fig. S3(b) in the ESM), indicating that the electrostatic force was not the crucial influence factor for \(\operatorname{Sb}(\mathrm{V})\) adsorption. The adsorption isothermal curves of three adsorbents indicated the adsorption capacity increased with the concentration augment at the initial stage \((100{-}800\ \mathrm{mg/L})\) and gradually \((900\ \mathrm{mg/L})\) reached adsorption equilibrium (Fig. 2). For three as-synthesized adsorbents with different metal centers, La-MGs achieved the maximum \(\operatorname{Sb}(\mathrm{V})\) adsorption capacity of \(897.6\ \mathrm{mg/g}\) at \(\mathrm{pH}\ 3.0\), which is 4.5 and 1.2 times higher than that of Mg-MGs and Zr-MGs, respectively, which was consistent with the calculated results from Riveted refinement that La-MGs obtained the maximum edge metal centers. The collected data analyzed by the Langmuir and Freundlich model suggested that Mg-MGs and Zr-MGs gave a better fit to the Freundlich model, whereas La-MGs better fitted the Langmuir model (Figs. S4(a) and 4(b) in the
ESM, and Table S2 in the ESM). Besides, kinetic experiments were conducted to further understand the effect of metal centers on Sb(V) removal rate, and the three absorbents were better fitted by pseudo-second-order models with detailed fitting parameters listed in Table S3 in the ESM, and Figs. S4(a) and S4(b) in the ESM. The Sb(V) removal rate followed the order of La-MGs \((625~\mathrm{mg/(g\cdot min)}) > \mathrm{Zr-MGs}\) \((502.5~\mathrm{mg/(g\cdot min)}) > \mathrm{Mg-MGs}\) \((53.4~\mathrm{mg/(g\cdot min)})\). Moreover, the metal centers utilization of the Mg-MGs, La-MGs, and Zr-MGs was \(20.4\%\), \(92.1\%\), and \(75\%\), respectively, which was consistent with the adsorption performance. On account of these analysis, the coordination environment of metal centers would be influential in the electronic structure of the adsorbents and then altered the adsorption capacity and removal rate of Sb(V).
3.3 The relationship between coordination environment and electronic structure of different adsorption sites
To further understand the relationship between the coordination environment and electronic structure for the adsorbents with different metal centers, the Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectra before and after Sb(V) adsorption were collected. Distinct peaks located at around 1,379 and \(1,538~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) corresponded to the symmetric and asymmetric vibrations of the –COOH groups derived from the coordinated terephthalate in the adsorbents [45], and these peaks were obviously diminished after Sb(V) adsorption (Figs. 3(a), 3(d), and 3(g)). A new peak attributed to Sb–O bond stretching vibration appeared at \(780~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) [46, 47], indicating that Sb(V) has been successfully adsorbed on the adsorbents. As observed in Figs. 3(b), 3(e), and 3(h), the Raman spectra of La-MGs exhibited a peak at \(856.8~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) attributed to La–O–La stretching mode [48], which showed red shifts after Sb(V) adsorption. This phenomenon also emerged in the Raman spectra
of Mg-MGs (Mg–O: \(645.0~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\)) and Zr-MGs (Zr–O: \(502.4~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\)) [49, 50], which diminished largely after Sb(V) adsorption. Furthermore, the absorbed MOFs exhibited ellipsoid structures, and the mapping results identified the elements of Mg, La, Zr, O, and Sb distributed uniformly in the three absorbents (Fig. S5 in the ESM), which suggested that the Sb(V) had been successfully anchored on the surface of three adsorbents. Thus, the M–O moieties in three adsorbents played vital roles on Sb(V) removal.
The effects of metal centers on Sb(V) removal performance were further supported by the Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET). As shown in Figs. S6(a) and S6(b) in the ESM, nitrogen physisorption measurements of Mg-MGs, La-MGs, and Zr-MGs exhibited Type IV isotherms at \(77~\mathrm{K}\), which were indicative of mesoporous materials with irregular opening holes that served as potential active sites for Sb(V) removal. Specifically, La-MGs with the maximum pore diameter suggested the formation of La-O-Sb(V) configuration within the least resistance, and La-MGs possessed highest BET surface area and largest pore volume, which enhanced the utilization of adsorption sites and boosted the adsorption performance (Table S5 in the ESM). The results verified that the diversity of metal centers affected the distribution of adsorption sites in the adsorbents. Furthermore, for the freshly prepared absorbents, the peaks at 531.0 and 835.0 eV corresponded to O 1s and La 3d (Fig. S6(c) in the ESM), and new peaks assigned to Sb 3d were clearly observed after the Sb(V) capture, indicating that antimony species were successfully immobilized on the surface of the adsorbents (Fig. S6(d) in the ESM). The binding energies of detailed O 1s spectrum at 530.8, 531.4, and \(533.1~\mathrm{eV}\) were assigned to oxygen bonded to metal (M–O), hydroxyl bonded to metal (M–OH), or surface-adsorbed oxygen [51–53], and adsorbed molecular water on three adsorbents, respectively (Figs. 3(c), 3(f), and 3(i)). The H–O peak was significantly stronger than that of M–O and H–O–H,
indicating the existence of abundant hydroxyl radicals or oxygen vacancy on the absorbents. After the uptake of Sb(V), the H–O ratio reduced for La-MGs (66.9% to 11.5%), Zr-MGs (67.8% to 15.3%), and Mg-MGs (79.9% to 74.4%), owing to the possible replacement of hydroxyl groups by Sb(OH)\(_6\) species during the adsorption process. In addition, La 3d spectrum in Fig. 4(b) exhibited two prominent bands at 836.7 and 853.0 eV belonged to La 3d\(_{5/2}\) and La 3d\(_{3/2}\). The binding energy of La 3d (0.2 eV) shifted to high-energy edge after Sb(V) adsorption, indicating an electron transfer in the valence band involving the reaction between Sb(V) and La-MGs [42]. The phenomenon also emerged in the Mg 1s (−1.8 eV) spectra of Mg-MGs (Fig. 4(a)) and Zr 3d (0.4 eV) spectra of Zr-MGs (Fig. 4(c)). These investigations verified that the diversity of metal centers affected the distribution of adsorption sites and electronic structure in the adsorbents, which further altered the adsorption for Sb(V) removal.
## 3.4 The investigation of original reasons through DFT calculations
Based on the Rietveld refinement results, the models of Mg-MGs, La-MGs, and Zr-MGs were designed and DFT calculations were employed to reveal the original reasons for Sb(V) removal diversity of MOFs-based adsorbents with different metal centers. It was found that the active sites of La and Zr atoms obtained larger blue region than Mg atoms, which was likely attacked by Sb(V) molecule (Fig. S7(a) in the ESM) [54, 55]. Moreover, Bader charge analysis also indicated that the charge density of La (2.2) and Zr (2.4) atoms of corresponding models was higher than that of Mg (1.7) in Mg-MGs [56], suggesting that d orbital electrons had influences on the adsorption performance.
To further understand the effect of d orbital electrons on Sb(V) removal, the adsorption energies \((E_{\mathrm{ads}})\) of Sb(V) on Mg-MGs, La-MGs, and Zr-MGs were calculated. As exhibited in Fig. 4(g) and
Fig. S7(b) in the ESM, the \(E_{\mathrm{ads}}\) values of La-MG \((-2.4\ \mathrm{eV})\) was lower compared with Mg-MGs \((-0.3\ \mathrm{eV})\) and Zr-MG \((-1.5\ \mathrm{eV})\), indicating that La-MGs was more stable in Sb(V) removal theoretically [16]. Moreover, the electronic structures of Mg-MGs, La-MGs, and Zr-MGs were proved by the charge density difference, PDOS and –COHP results. Figure 4(e) and Fig. S7(c) in the ESM revealed that the charge density difference of La-MGs-Sb(V) at the interface was obviously redistributed [23], suggesting the interaction between La-MGs and Sb(V), in accordance with the X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) results. According to the Bader charge \((q)\) results, the electron transfer \((-0.9\ \mathrm{e})\) from La-MGs to Sb(V) would take place on the interface of La-MGs-Sb(V), which formed strong interaction force to low the adsorption energy and enhance the Sb(V) removal performance. Comparatively less charge transfers between Sb(V) molecules and other two absorbents were determined, where \(-0.1\ \mathrm{e}\) for Mg-MGs, and \(-0.5\ \mathrm{e}\) for Zr-MGs, which was in accordance with the calculated Eads results (Fig. 4(g) and Fig. S7(b) in the ESM). To further elucidate the phenomenon of the bonding nature, a partial density of state (PDOS) analysis was further performed. As exhibited in Fig. S8 in the ESM, the contribution of the DOS near the Fermi energy of the Sb(V) molecules was mainly derived from the \(5\mathrm{s}^{2}\) and \(5\mathrm{p}^{3}\) orbitals of Sb, which was consistent with the previous reported results [57]. Intriguingly, La-MGs obtained higher content of electron state around the Fermi energy between the La \(5\mathrm{d}^{1}\) and \(6\mathrm{s}^{2}\) orbitals of the La nodes after Sb(V) adsorption when compared with Mg-MGs (Mg: \(3\mathrm{s}^{1}\)) and Zr-MGs (Zr: \(4\mathrm{d}^{2}\) and \(5\mathrm{s}^{2}\)) [56] (Fig. 4(i) and Fig. S8 in the ESM), revealing that La–O–Sb interaction in La-MGs was the most stable among three absorbents, which was in accordance with the calculated \(E_{\mathrm{ads}}\) results and differential charge density results. Additionally, the bonding contribution of La–O (0.223) bonds from the crystal orbital Hamiltonian population (–COHP) results in La-MGs were
greater than that of \(\mathrm{Mg}\) -MGs (Mg–O: 0.0151) and \(Z\mathbf{r}\) -MGs (Zr–O: 0.0999) [58] (Fig. 4(h)), which was more favorable for the Sb(V) adsorption. These results therefore confirmed that La-MGs effectively drove the charge transfer during adsorption process, reduced the adsorption energy and increased the content of electron state around the Fermi energy in La \(5\mathrm{d}^{1}\) and \(6\mathbf{s}^{2}\) orbits, and eventually enhanced the Sb(V) adsorption performance. Accordingly, the mechanism of the high Sb(V) adsorption performance of La-MGs as compared to \(\mathrm{Mg}\) -MGs and \(Z\mathbf{r}\) -MGs was that La-MGs obtained more edge adsorption sites and enhanced bonding interaction with Sb(V), which jointly promoted the Sb(V) removal and the removal mechanism was depicted in Fig. 5(a).
## 3.5 Practical implication
The potential environmental applications of La-MGs were finally evaluated by two practical water samples from Tianyi Lake and Ganjiang River with initial Sb concentration of 380 and \(53~\upmu\mathrm{g/L}\) , respectively (Table S6 in the ESM). During the batch experiments, all samples exhibited a pronounced effect of Sb capture. As depicted in Fig. 5(b), the residual Sb concentration detected in Tianyi Lake samples was \(0.096~\mathrm{mg/L},\) which was lower than the wastewater discharge standard of \(0.1~\mathrm{mg/L}\) . Intriguingly, La-MGs exhibited optimal removal efficiency of \(95.7\%\) for Ganjiang River samples, and the residual Sb concentration of \(2.3~\mathrm{ug/L}\) detected was fully compliant with the WHO standards of \(6\,\mathrm{ug/L}\) (Fig. 5(c)). Overall, our study provided a feasible and scalable way to develop MOF-based adsorbents with adsorption functionality for practical water purification.
## 4 Conclusion
Our experimental and theoretical studies have clearly demonstrated that a class of metal-based MOF materials acts as
not only very promising adsorbents for Sb(V) removal but also a good testbed for fundamental relations between electronic structure and adsorption activity. The Sb(V) adsorption performance was found to follow the decreasing sequence La\(\mathrm{MGs}\,>\,\mathrm{Zr-MGs}\,>\,\mathrm{Mg-MGs},\) and La-MGs achieved optimum adsorption capacity of \(897.6~\mathrm{mg/g}\) . Moreover, the sites utilization of La-MGs was enhanced from \(20.4\%\) to \(92.1\%\) , attributing to the higher surface area and larger volume that exposed more edge adsorption sites. DFT calculations indicated that the enhanced Sb(V) removal performance was derived from lower adsorption energy, which efficiently drove the charge transfer during the adsorption process and increased the content of electron state around the Fermi energy in La \(5\mathrm{d}^{1}\) and \(6\mathbf{s}^{2}\) orbits. Moreover, LaMGs effectively captured antimony at low concentration, reaching the wastewater discharge standard and drinking water standard for water samples from Tianyi Lake and Ganjiang River, respectively. This work might shed novel insights on the rational design of the local coordination structures of MOFs-based materials for enhanced metal oxyanion removal.
